## Networking Concepts

### HTTP

The main application-layer protocol used during this room was:

```text
HTTP
```

HTTP allows clients and web servers to communicate using requests and responses.

### Port 80

The IIS web server was running on:

```text
TCP Port 80
```

Port 80 is commonly associated with unencrypted HTTP web traffic.

### TCP

HTTP uses TCP as its transport protocol.

Before HTTP communication occurs, TCP establishes a connection using the three-way handshake:

```text
Client → SYN
Server → SYN-ACK
Client → ACK
```

Once the connection is established, HTTP requests and responses can travel between the systems.

### HTTP Methods

Common HTTP methods include:

```text
GET
POST
HEAD
```

WebDAV extends HTTP with additional methods such as:

```text
PUT
DELETE
COPY
MOVE
PROPFIND
LOCK
UNLOCK
```

The `PUT` method was important during this room because it allowed a file to be uploaded to the WebDAV directory.

### WebDAV

WebDAV stands for:

```text
Web Distributed Authoring and Versioning
```

It extends HTTP and allows users to remotely manage files on a web server.

If WebDAV is misconfigured, an attacker may be able to upload, modify, move, or delete files.

### Reverse Shell

Before obtaining the reverse shell, my attacking machine was sending HTTP requests to the target:

```text
Attacker → HTTP Request → Target IIS Server
```

With a reverse shell, the direction changed:

```text
Target Windows Server → TCP Connection → Attacker's Listener
```

The compromised machine initiated a connection back to my Netcat listener, giving me an interactive shell.

