## Vulnerabilities / Findings

### WebDAV Enabled

WebDAV was enabled on the IIS server and exposed several potentially dangerous HTTP methods.

### Dangerous HTTP Methods

Methods such as:

```text
PUT
DELETE
COPY
MOVE
```

increased the attack surface because they allowed files and resources on the server to be modified.

### Directory Listing Enabled

Directory listing allowed files stored inside web directories to be viewed.

This helped expose sensitive files that should not have been publicly accessible.

### Sensitive Files Exposed

Files such as:

```text
webdav_notes.txt
config.bak
web.config
```

were accessible through the web server.

### Credential Exposure

The WebDAV notes contained credentials that could be used to authenticate to the WebDAV directory.

### Executable File Upload

WebDAV allowed an ASPX file to be uploaded.

Because IIS could execute ASP.NET files, the uploaded ASPX file could execute commands on the server.

### Remote Command Execution

The uploaded ASPX command shell allowed commands such as:

```cmd
whoami
```

to execute on the Windows target.

This resulted in remote command execution.

