## What I Learned

This room helped me understand how different web server weaknesses can be chained together during a penetration test.

I learned how to:

- Scan a web server with Nmap.
- Identify services and versions running on TCP port 80.
- Fingerprint Microsoft IIS and ASP.NET.
- Enumerate supported HTTP methods.
- Identify WebDAV functionality.
- Understand WebDAV methods such as `PUT` and `PROPFIND`.
- Perform IIS short-name enumeration.
- Discover exposed directories and sensitive files.
- Understand the risks of exposed backup and configuration files.
- Identify credential exposure.
- Authenticate to WebDAV.
- Understand how WebDAV can allow file uploads.
- Turn an executable ASPX upload into remote command execution.
- Understand how a reverse shell works.
- Enumerate Windows privileges after gaining access.
- Understand how HTTP communicates over TCP.

The biggest lesson from this room was that a system does not always need one major vulnerability to become compromised.

Several smaller weaknesses can be chained together:

```text
Information Disclosure
        +
Credential Exposure
        +
WebDAV Misconfiguration
        +
Executable File Upload
        =
Remote Command Execution
```

This room showed why enumeration is important because each piece of information discovered helped lead to the next stage of the attack.